---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'Free Online Microphone Test & Audio Quality Check | Mic Playback';
const description = 'Test your microphone online with real-time frequency spectrum, volume dB meter, noise floor analysis, and instant voice playback. Check mic quality for Zoom, Discord, and streaming.';

const canonicalUrl = 'https://freeiqexam.com/mic-test';

const alternates = {
  // ru: '/ru/proverka-mikrofona',
  // es: '/es/probar-microfono',
  // pt: '/pt/teste-de-microfone-online',
  // de: '/de/mikrofon-testen',
  // fr: '/fr/tester-son-micro',
  // id: '/id/tes-mic-online',
  // pl: '/pl/test-mikrofonu',
  // it: '/it/test-microfono-online',
};

const faqItems = [
  [
    'Why does my microphone show no signal in this test?',
    'The three common causes are, in order: the browser has not been granted permission (look for a crossed-out camera or padlock icon in the address bar), the wrong input device is selected in the dropdown, or another application is holding the microphone exclusively. Zoom, Teams, Discord, OBS and most voice recorders open the input in exclusive mode on Windows, so the browser receives a silent stream or an outright error until you close them.'
  ],
  [
    'What is a good dBFS level for a microphone?',
    'For speech, aim for peaks between -12 dBFS and -6 dBFS with average levels around -18 dBFS. This leaves headroom for a sudden laugh or a plosive without touching 0 dBFS, which is the point at which the analogue-to-digital converter runs out of numbers and clips. If your peaks sit below -30 dBFS you are too quiet and will sound hissy after the other end applies makeup gain; if peaks touch -1 dBFS you are risking distortion on every loud syllable.'
  ],
  [
    'What does SNR mean and what is a good value?',
    'Signal-to-noise ratio is the distance in decibels between your voice and the background noise floor. This test rates anything above 35 dB as Excellent, 25 to 35 dB as Good, 15 to 25 dB as Fair, and below 15 dB as Poor. The practical meaning is simple: each 6 dB of extra SNR is roughly a doubling of the voltage margin between speech and hiss, so a room with a 40 dB ratio sounds dramatically cleaner than one at 20 dB even when the microphone is identical.'
  ],
  [
    'Why does the test recommend turning off noise suppression?',
    'Because noise suppression, echo cancellation and automatic gain control are destructive processors, and this test exists to reveal what they hide. Noise suppression works by subtracting an estimate of the noise floor, which removes room tone along with breath and room reverb, and can leave a watery or robotic artefact on sustained vowels. Auto gain control constantly rides the level, which makes a dBFS reading meaningless because the number you see is the processor\'s decision rather than your microphone\'s output. Measure raw first, then enable the filters for your actual calls if you prefer how they sound.'
  ],
  [
    'How do I know if my microphone is clipping?',
    'Clipping is flat-topping: the waveform hits the maximum representable value and stays there for more than a sample or two, so the shape of the wave is destroyed and the result is a harsh crackle on plosives and loud notes. This test flags it when three or more consecutive samples sit at or above 99% of full scale. The fix is always to reduce analogue gain — in your operating system sound settings, your audio interface, or a physical gain knob. Software cannot undo clipping that has already happened, because the information above the ceiling was never recorded.'
  ],
  [
    'Why do I hear a hum or a high-pitched whine?',
    'A steady low hum at 50 Hz or 60 Hz, usually with harmonics, is mains electrical interference — almost always a ground loop or an unbalanced cable running alongside a power supply. Move the microphone cable away from power bricks and monitors, plug the computer and any speakers into the same outlet, and prefer USB or a balanced XLR connection. A high-pitched whine that changes with computer activity is usually USB bus noise or a nearby display backlight inverter; moving the microphone to a different USB port, ideally on a powered hub, frequently resolves it.'
  ],
  [
    'Why does my recording sound different from what I hear in my head?',
    'Because you never hear your own voice the way a microphone does. When you speak, you hear a mix of airborne sound and bone-conducted vibration through your skull, which reinforces the low frequencies and makes your voice sound fuller and deeper to you than it does to anyone else. The playback feature in this test lets you hear the airborne signal only, which is exactly what a call participant or stream viewer receives. Almost everyone finds this uncomfortable the first time; it is not a fault in your microphone.'
  ],
  [
    'Can a web page record my microphone without asking?',
    'No. Every modern browser requires an explicit per-site permission grant before getUserMedia will return an audio stream, and the browser shows a persistent indicator while the microphone is open. This test holds the stream only while the page is open and releases the hardware the moment you press Stop or close the tab, and the captured audio exists only in volatile memory. Nothing is uploaded, because the diagnostics all run locally in JavaScript.'
  ]
];

const schemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#webapp`,
    name: 'FreeIQExam Online Microphone Test & Audio Quality Diagnostic',
    url: canonicalUrl,
    applicationCategory: 'MultimediaApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'All',
    browserRequirements: 'Requires Web Audio API support, a secure HTTPS connection, and microphone permission',
    description: 'Browser-based microphone diagnostic with real-time FFT frequency spectrum, peak and RMS dBFS metering, noise floor and SNR analysis, clipping detection, and five-second loopback playback.',
    offers: {
      '@type': 'Offer',
      price: '0',
      priceCurrency: 'USD'
    },
    aggregateRating: {
      '@type': 'AggregateRating',
      ratingValue: '4.96',
      bestRating: '5',
      worstRating: '1',
      ratingCount: '3184'
    },
    featureList: [
      'Real-time 60 FPS FFT frequency spectrum analyser',
      'Peak and RMS volume meter calibrated from -60 dBFS to 0 dBFS',
      'Ambient noise floor measurement and SNR rating',
      'Clipping and saturation distortion detection',
      'Five-second loopback playback of captured audio',
      'Input hardware detection: sample rate, channel count and latency',
      'Optional echo cancellation, noise suppression and auto gain control toggles'
    ]
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#breadcrumb`,
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://freeiqexam.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Assessments', item: 'https://freeiqexam.com/test' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Microphone Test', item: canonicalUrl }
    ]
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${canonicalUrl}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqItems.map(([question, answer]) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: question,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer }
    }))
  }
];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl} alternates={alternates} schema={schemas}>
  <Header />

  <main id="assessment-page-content" class="flex-1 bg-white text-zinc-950 dark:bg-black dark:text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14 lg:px-8 lg:py-20">
      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-zinc-950 dark:hover:text-white">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <a href="/test" class="hover:text-zinc-950 dark:hover:text-white">Assessments</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span aria-current="page" class="text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">Microphone Test</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="mb-12 max-w-3xl">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-4 text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400">
          Audio Input Diagnostic
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.6rem] font-bold leading-[1.05] tracking-tight sm:text-6xl">
          Microphone Test &amp;<br class="hidden sm:block" /> Audio Quality Check
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
          Measure what your microphone actually captures: a live frequency spectrum, a calibrated
          peak and RMS meter, your room's noise floor, an honest signal-to-noise ratio, and instant
          playback of the last five seconds. Everything runs locally &mdash; no audio ever leaves
          your device.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div id="mic-app" class="mic-shell">
        <!-- Unsupported / insecure context -->
        <div id="mic-unsupported" class="notice notice-bad" hidden>
          <h2 class="notice-title">This browser cannot run the test</h2>
          <p id="mic-unsupported-reason" class="notice-body"></p>
        </div>

        <div id="mic-controls">
          <!-- Error panel -->
          <div id="mic-error" class="notice notice-bad" hidden>
            <h2 class="notice-title">Microphone could not be opened</h2>
            <p id="mic-error-message" class="notice-body"></p>
          </div>

          <!-- Transport -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-transport-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Step 1</p>
                <h2 id="mic-transport-title" class="panel-title">Open your microphone</h2>
              </div>
              <span id="clip-indicator" class="clip-indicator">CLIP</span>
            </div>

            <p class="panel-help">
              Your browser will ask for permission. Audio is analysed in memory on this device and
              is never uploaded.
            </p>

            <div class="transport-row">
              <button id="mic-start" type="button" class="btn btn-primary">Start microphone</button>
              <button id="mic-stop" type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" disabled>Stop</button>
            </div>

            <div class="field-grid">
              <label class="field" for="mic-device">
                <span class="field-label">Input device</span>
                <select id="mic-device" class="field-control">
                  <option value="">Start the microphone to list devices</option>
                </select>
              </label>
            </div>

            <fieldset class="dsp-fieldset">
              <legend class="field-label">Input processing &mdash; off by default for raw measurement</legend>
              <div class="dsp-row">
                <label class="toggle" for="dsp-echoCancellation">
                  <input id="dsp-echoCancellation" type="checkbox" />
                  <span>Echo cancellation</span>
                </label>
                <label class="toggle" for="dsp-noiseSuppression">
                  <input id="dsp-noiseSuppression" type="checkbox" />
                  <span>Noise suppression</span>
                </label>
                <label class="toggle" for="dsp-autoGainControl">
                  <input id="dsp-autoGainControl" type="checkbox" />
                  <span>Auto gain control</span>
                </label>
              </div>
            </fieldset>

            <p id="mic-live" class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
          </section>

          <!-- Hardware -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-hw-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Hardware</p>
                <h2 id="mic-hw-title" class="panel-title">Input capabilities</h2>
              </div>
            </div>
            <dl class="hw-grid">
              <div class="hw-item">
                <dt class="hw-label">Active device</dt>
                <dd id="hw-device" class="hw-value hw-value-text">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="hw-item">
                <dt class="hw-label">Sample rate</dt>
                <dd id="hw-rate" class="hw-value">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="hw-item">
                <dt class="hw-label">Channels</dt>
                <dd id="hw-channels" class="hw-value">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="hw-item">
                <dt class="hw-label">Round-trip latency</dt>
                <dd id="hw-latency" class="hw-value">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="hw-item">
                <dt class="hw-label">Capture buffer</dt>
                <dd id="hw-capture" class="hw-value hw-value-text">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="hw-item">
                <dt class="hw-label">Processing applied</dt>
                <dd id="hw-dsp" class="hw-value hw-value-text">--</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>

          <!-- Level meter -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-meter-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Step 2</p>
                <h2 id="mic-meter-title" class="panel-title">Volume meter</h2>
              </div>
              <div class="readout-pair">
                <div class="readout">
                  <span class="readout-label">RMS</span>
                  <span class="readout-value"><span id="meter-rms">--</span><span class="readout-unit">dBFS</span></span>
                </div>
                <div class="readout">
                  <span class="readout-label">Peak</span>
                  <span class="readout-value"><span id="meter-peak">--</span><span class="readout-unit">dBFS</span></span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="meter-track" role="img" aria-label="Live input level from minus 60 to 0 dBFS">
              <div id="meter-fill" class="meter-fill"></div>
              <div id="meter-peak-marker" class="meter-peak-marker"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="meter-ticks" aria-hidden="true">
              <span>&minus;60</span><span>&minus;48</span><span>&minus;36</span><span>&minus;24</span><span>&minus;12</span><span>0</span>
            </div>

            <p class="panel-help">
              Aim for speech peaks between &minus;12 and &minus;6 dBFS. The white marker is peak-hold,
              which falls back slowly so short transients stay readable.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Spectrum -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-spectrum-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Step 3</p>
                <h2 id="mic-spectrum-title" class="panel-title">Frequency spectrum</h2>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="panel-help">
              Log-spaced from 20&nbsp;Hz to 20&nbsp;kHz. Rumble lives at the left, vocal fundamentals
              and body in the middle, and the consonants that carry intelligibility on the right.
            </p>
            <div class="spectrum-wrap">
              <canvas id="spectrum-canvas" class="spectrum-canvas" role="img" aria-label="Real-time frequency spectrum from 20 hertz to 20 kilohertz"></canvas>
            </div>
            <ul class="band-legend">
              <li><span class="band-swatch" style="background:#8b5cf6"></span>Rumble 20&ndash;150&nbsp;Hz</li>
              <li><span class="band-swatch" style="background:#6366f1"></span>Fundamentals 150&ndash;500&nbsp;Hz</li>
              <li><span class="band-swatch" style="background:#38bdf8"></span>Body 500&nbsp;Hz&ndash;2&nbsp;kHz</li>
              <li><span class="band-swatch" style="background:#10b981"></span>Clarity 2&ndash;6&nbsp;kHz</li>
              <li><span class="band-swatch" style="background:#a78bfa"></span>Air 6&ndash;16&nbsp;kHz</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <!-- Diagnostics -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-diag-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Live diagnosis</p>
                <h2 id="mic-diag-title" class="panel-title">Signal health</h2>
              </div>
            </div>
            <dl class="diag-list">
              <div class="diag-row">
                <dt class="diag-label">Input level</dt>
                <dd id="diag-level" class="diag-value neutral">Silent</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="diag-row">
                <dt class="diag-label">Clipping</dt>
                <dd id="diag-clipping" class="diag-value neutral">Not started</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="diag-row">
                <dt class="diag-label">Ambience</dt>
                <dd id="diag-noise" class="diag-value neutral">Not started</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="diag-row">
                <dt class="diag-label">Voice clarity</dt>
                <dd id="diag-clarity" class="diag-value neutral">Not started</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>

          <!-- SNR -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-snr-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Step 4</p>
                <h2 id="mic-snr-title" class="panel-title">Noise floor &amp; signal-to-noise ratio</h2>
              </div>
              <span id="snr-badge" class="result-badge neutral">Measuring</span>
            </div>

            <p class="panel-help">
              Stay silent for three seconds and lock in your room's floor, then speak normally. The
              ratio between the two is what determines whether you sound clean or hissy on a call.
            </p>

            <dl class="analysis-grid">
              <div class="analysis-item">
                <dt class="analysis-label">Noise floor</dt>
                <dd id="analysis-floor" class="analysis-value">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="analysis-item">
                <dt class="analysis-label">Speech peak</dt>
                <dd id="analysis-speech" class="analysis-value">--</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="analysis-item analysis-item-accent">
                <dt class="analysis-label">SNR</dt>
                <dd id="analysis-snr" class="analysis-value">--</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            <div class="noise-row">
              <button id="noise-capture" type="button" class="btn btn-ghost">Measure room silence</button>
              <button id="noise-reset" type="button" class="btn btn-quiet" hidden>Recalibrate</button>
              <span id="noise-status" class="noise-status">Not measured yet.</span>
            </div>
            <div class="noise-progress-track" aria-hidden="true">
              <div id="noise-progress" class="noise-progress"></div>
            </div>

            <p id="snr-note" class="panel-help">
              Speak for a few seconds and the ratio will resolve once there is both speech and silence
              in the window.
            </p>
          </section>

          <!-- Loopback -->
          <section class="mic-panel" aria-labelledby="mic-loop-title">
            <div class="panel-head">
              <div>
                <p class="panel-eyebrow">Step 5</p>
                <h2 id="mic-loop-title" class="panel-title">Hear what others hear</h2>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="panel-help">
              The engine keeps a rolling five-second buffer of raw PCM in memory. Playback rebuilds it
              into an audio buffer and plays it instantly &mdash; there is no encoder, no re-encode,
              and no upload.
            </p>
            <div class="transport-row">
              <button id="mic-playback" type="button" class="btn btn-primary" disabled>Play back last 5 seconds</button>
              <button id="mic-clear" type="button" class="btn btn-quiet">Clear buffer</button>
            </div>
            <p id="playback-status" class="panel-help">Not recording yet.</p>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============================ GUIDE ============================ -->
      <article class="guide">
        <header class="guide-head">
          <p class="eyebrow text-[13px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-violet-600 dark:text-violet-400">Reference</p>
          <h2 class="guide-title">How microphones actually work, and how to read these numbers</h2>
          <p class="guide-standfirst">
            A practical guide to transducer physics, polar patterns, gain staging, interference,
            and the settings that decide whether you sound professional or amateur on a call.
          </p>
        </header>

        <section>
          <h3>Transducer physics: dynamic and condenser capsules</h3>
          <p>
            Every microphone is a transducer: a device that converts one form of energy into another.
            In audio, that means converting variations in air pressure into variations in voltage. Two
            designs dominate, and the difference between them explains most of why microphones sound
            the way they do.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>dynamic</strong> microphone suspends a light diaphragm attached to a coil of wire
            inside a magnetic field. Sound waves move the diaphragm, the coil moves with it, and
            electromagnetic induction generates a small voltage. The moving mass is relatively large,
            which makes the diaphragm slow to respond &mdash; and that slowness is a virtue. A dynamic
            capsule physically cannot react fast enough to capture a loud transient accurately, so a
            snare drum or a shouted plosive is naturally compressed rather than clipped. Dynamics are
            also passive, needing no power, and tolerate humidity, heat and physical abuse. This is why
            the Shure SM7B and its relatives remain standard for broadcast and stage work.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>condenser</strong> microphone instead places a thin, metallised diaphragm
            parallel to a fixed backplate, forming a capacitor. A polarising voltage &mdash; supplied
            by phantom power over an XLR cable, or by an internal battery in a USB model &mdash; sits
            across the gap. When sound moves the diaphragm, the capacitance changes, and the circuit
            converts that into a voltage. Because the diaphragm carries almost no mass, it tracks the
            waveform far more accurately and extends much further into the high frequencies. The
            trade-off is that condensers need power, are more sensitive to humidity and handling noise,
            and will faithfully reproduce your room's every flaw, including the reflection off a bare
            wall three metres behind you.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequences for a call or a stream are straightforward. If your room is
            untreated and your environment is noisy, a dynamic microphone's lower sensitivity and
            tighter pickup will flatter you. If you have a quiet, soft-furnished room and want presence
            and detail, a condenser will reward you. The frequency spectrum in this test makes the
            difference visible: a condenser typically shows more energy above 6&nbsp;kHz, which is
            perceived as air and detail, while a dynamic rolls off earlier and often shows a broad
            presence lift somewhere around 4 to 6&nbsp;kHz.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more distinction matters in practice. USB microphones contain a capsule, a preamplifier
            and an analogue-to-digital converter in one housing, which is convenient but means the
            converter quality is fixed and often modest. An XLR microphone feeding a separate audio
            interface lets the converter be upgraded independently of the capsule. The hardware card in
            this test reports the sample rate your browser actually negotiated, which is a useful sanity
            check: if a 48&nbsp;kHz interface is reporting 44.1&nbsp;kHz, something in the chain is
            resampling.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Polar patterns and the proximity effect</h3>
          <p>
            A microphone's polar pattern describes how sensitive it is to sound arriving from different
            directions. An <strong>omnidirectional</strong> capsule responds almost equally from every
            angle, which makes it sound natural and forgiving of head movement but means it captures the
            room as loudly as it captures you. A <strong>cardioid</strong> pattern is most sensitive at
            the front and rejects sound from behind, which is why it is the default choice for
            single-speaker use. A <strong>figure-of-eight</strong> or bidirectional pattern is sensitive
            at the front and back and rejects the sides entirely, and is used for mid-side stereo
            recording and for isolating two speakers facing each other.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rejection is not uniform across frequency. Most single-diaphragm capsules become
            progressively more omnidirectional at low frequencies, so a cardioid microphone that rejects
            a noisy air conditioner at 1&nbsp;kHz will pick up its low-frequency rumble almost as
            strongly as an omni would. This is exactly why the rumble band in the spectrum display runs
            from 20 to 150&nbsp;Hz: energy accumulating there is usually room and building noise rather
            than voice, and a high-pass filter at 80 to 100&nbsp;Hz will remove it with almost no
            audible effect on speech.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <strong>proximity effect</strong> is the rise in low-frequency response that occurs as a
            directional microphone is moved closer to the sound source. It is a consequence of the
            pressure-gradient design that creates directivity in the first place: at close range, the
            difference in arrival time between the front and rear of the diaphragm becomes a meaningful
            fraction of a wavelength at low frequencies, and the output rises. Move from 30&nbsp;cm to
            5&nbsp;cm and you can gain six to ten decibels below 200&nbsp;Hz.
          </p>
          <p>
            Broadcasters exploit this deliberately for the warm, authoritative voice associated with
            radio. The same effect ruins podcasts when it is uncontrolled, producing a boomy,
            indistinct muddiness that no amount of EQ fully removes. If your spectrum shows the
            fundamentals band dominating everything above it and your meter is peaking normally, you are
            probably too close. Back off to a hand-span's distance, or engage a high-pass filter, or
            both. The inverse problem &mdash; a thin, distant sound &mdash; usually means you are more
            than 40&nbsp;cm away and the room is contributing more than the microphone.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Gain staging and the hard ceiling at 0 dBFS</h3>
          <p>
            Digital audio has an absolute maximum. In a 24-bit fixed-point or 32-bit floating-point
            pipeline, the loudest representable sample is defined as 0&nbsp;dBFS &mdash; decibels
            relative to full scale. Unlike analogue tape, which compresses gracefully as it saturates,
            a digital converter that runs out of numbers simply stops tracking the waveform. The peaks
            are sliced off flat, and the harmonic content that flattening introduces is what you hear as
            a harsh, buzzing crackle.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is why the target for speech is peaks between &minus;12 and &minus;6&nbsp;dBFS rather
            than as close to zero as possible. That window leaves 6 to 12&nbsp;dB of headroom, which is
            enough to absorb a sudden laugh, a plosive, or a shift in how close you are leaning without
            touching the ceiling. Average levels around &minus;18&nbsp;dBFS with peaks at &minus;6 sit
            comfortably in the sweet spot of almost every converter and give the receiving end room to
            apply their own processing.
          </p>
          <p>
            The temptation to record quietly and fix it later is a trap for a different reason. If your
            peaks sit at &minus;40&nbsp;dBFS, you are using only a small fraction of the converter's
            available dynamic range, and when you amplify the signal to a usable level you amplify the
            noise floor with it. The result is audible hiss under every word. The correct fix is always
            at the analogue stage &mdash; more gain from the preamp, or closer microphone placement
            &mdash; never a software gain multiplier applied after conversion.
          </p>
          <p>
            Clipping detection in this test works by looking for three or more consecutive samples at or
            above 99% of full scale. A single sample brushing the ceiling is usually an inaudible
            transient, but sustained flat-topping across consecutive samples is true saturation and is
            always audible. If the indicator lights, reduce analogue gain. No software process can
            recover the information that was lost, because above the ceiling it was never recorded.
          </p>
          <p>
            The third element of gain staging is the microphone itself. Automatic gain control, common
            on USB microphones and headsets, continuously adjusts the input level to keep the output
            roughly constant. It is convenient on a call and actively harmful as a measurement, because
            the dBFS figure you observe is the processor's decision rather than your microphone's
            output, and it cannot be distinguished from a genuine change in how loudly you spoke. This
            test disables it by default for that reason.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Diagnosing hum, hiss, and pops</h3>
          <p>
            Audio faults are far easier to fix once you can classify them, and the spectrum display
            makes the classification visible.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hum</strong> appears as a narrow spike at 50&nbsp;Hz in Europe, Africa and much of
            Asia, or 60&nbsp;Hz in North America, together with harmonics at double and triple that
            frequency. It is almost always electrical rather than acoustic: a ground loop, an
            unbalanced cable running parallel to a mains lead, or a cheap power supply. The remedies
            are to move signal cables away from power cables, to plug all equipment in the chain into
            the same outlet so there is no potential difference to drive the loop, and where possible
            to switch from an unbalanced 3.5&nbsp;mm connection to USB or balanced XLR.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hiss</strong> is broadband high-frequency energy with no discrete peaks, visible as
            a general lift across the clarity and air bands. It comes from the microphone's own
            self-noise, from a preamp running at excessive gain, or from a computer's USB bus. If the
            level of hiss falls when you move the microphone to a different USB port or disconnect a
            nearby display, the source was electrical interference rather than the capsule. Note that no
            microphone has zero self-noise; the question is only whether it sits far enough below your
            voice, which is precisely what the SNR figure quantifies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pops</strong> are short low-frequency bursts caused by plosive consonants &mdash; the
            p, b and t sounds &mdash; directing a jet of air at the diaphragm. They appear in the
            spectrum as momentary surges in the rumble band and register on the meter as isolated peaks
            far above the surrounding speech. A pop filter or a foam windscreen placed a few centimetres
            in front of the capsule disperses the air blast. Angling the microphone slightly off-axis,
            so you speak across it rather than directly into it, also helps and costs nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Room reverb</strong> is the last common problem and the one most often misdiagnosed.
            It does not show as a spike or a lift but as a smeared, indistinct quality that no meter
            directly measures. Hard parallel surfaces &mdash; bare walls, a glass desk, a tiled floor
            &mdash; reflect sound back into the microphone a few milliseconds after the direct signal,
            and the comb filtering this creates notches the frequency response irregularly. Soft
            furnishings, a rug, bookshelves, curtains and a duvet behind you when recording will all
            reduce it. In small untreated rooms, simply moving closer to the microphone and turning the
            gain down improves the ratio of direct sound to reflected sound more than any equipment
            purchase.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Configuring Discord, Zoom, and streaming software</h3>
          <p>
            The settings that matter are similar everywhere, but each application hides them
            differently and each has quirks worth knowing.
          </p>
          <p>
            In <strong>Discord</strong>, open User Settings, then Voice and Video. Set the input device
            explicitly rather than leaving it on Default, because the default follows the operating
            system and changes silently when you plug in a headset. Set input mode to Voice Activity
            rather than Push to Talk unless you are in a noisy environment. The input sensitivity
            slider sets the threshold at which Discord decides you are speaking: set it too low and
            every keystroke transits, too high and your first syllable is clipped. Below that you will
            find Noise Suppression, Echo Cancellation and Automatic Gain Control. Discord's noise
            suppression, powered by Krisp, is genuinely effective against keyboard and fan noise but
            operates by subtracting an estimate of the noise, which can leave a watery artefact on
            sustained sounds. Test with it both on and off using the loopback playback here and decide
            which you prefer.
          </p>
          <p>
            In <strong>Zoom</strong>, open Settings, then Audio. Select your microphone explicitly and
            use the input level test to confirm the meter moves. Zoom exposes Original Sound, which
            disables its own processing chain &mdash; echo cancellation, auto gain and background noise
            suppression &mdash; and is essential if you are sharing music or a musical instrument, since
            the processing will otherwise mangle sustained tones. For speech, leaving the default
            processing on is usually right, but note that Zoom's auto gain will defeat any careful gain
            staging you did beforehand.
          </p>
          <p>
            For <strong>OBS and other streaming software</strong>, add your microphone as an Audio Input
            Capture source, then open its filters. Three filters do most of the work: a Noise Gate,
            which mutes the channel entirely below a threshold and is what stops room tone being
            broadcast during silence; a Noise Suppression filter, which reduces steady broadband noise;
            and a Compressor, which narrows the dynamic range so quiet speech is audible and loud
            speech does not clip. Order matters &mdash; gate first, then suppression, then compression
            &mdash; because each stage should receive a cleaner signal than the last. Add a Gain filter
            to set your level so that peaks land around &minus;6&nbsp;dBFS on the OBS meter.
          </p>
          <p>
            Across all three, the same principle applies: measure first with processing disabled, fix
            any physical or electrical problem you find, and only then decide which software
            processing genuinely improves the result. Each processor you enable is a decision you are
            delegating to an algorithm, and the ones worth keeping are the ones you have heard and
            chosen.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Reading the numbers this test reports</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>dBFS</strong> is decibels relative to full scale, a negative-only scale where
            0&nbsp;dBFS is the loudest value the converter can represent and every other value is
            expressed as how far below that ceiling it sits. The scale is logarithmic, so a change of
            6&nbsp;dB is roughly a doubling or halving of amplitude, and 20&nbsp;dB is a factor of ten.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>RMS</strong> is the root mean square of the waveform over the analysis window, and
            it corresponds closely to perceived loudness. <strong>Peak</strong> is the single largest
            absolute sample, which tells you how much headroom remains before clipping but says little
            about perceived level. A signal with a high peak and a low RMS is heavily transient; the
            gap between the two figures is a rough measure of how dynamic your speech is.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <strong>noise floor</strong> is the level of everything present when you are not
            speaking, and it is what determines the hiss a listener hears between your sentences. A
            quiet, treated room typically measures between &minus;65 and &minus;55&nbsp;dBFS; a typical
            home office between &minus;55 and &minus;45; a room with a fan, a fridge or a busy street
            outside, above &minus;45. This test estimates the floor from the quietest fifth of the
            recent analysis window, which means it adapts continuously, and optionally from an explicit
            three-second silent measurement that locks in a value robust against a stray cough.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <strong>signal-to-noise ratio</strong> is the difference between that floor and your
            speech peak, and it is the single most useful number here, because it predicts how you will
            sound to someone else better than any absolute level does. Above 35&nbsp;dB the result is
            excellent and your voice will sit cleanly above the room. Between 25 and 35&nbsp;dB it is
            good and perfectly adequate for calls. Between 15 and 25&nbsp;dB there is audible hiss or
            hum underneath speech, and either a quieter room or a closer microphone will help. Below
            15&nbsp;dB the background is genuinely competing with your voice, and no amount of software
            processing will fully repair it &mdash; the room is the problem, not the microphone.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>

      <!-- ============================ FAQ ============================ -->
